 from being trained on optimal subsets of annotated data. Active learning algorithms effectively achieve this by pinpointing data samples that are essential for improving DNN performance. The method is particularly advantageous in large-scale industrial settings where labeling costs are exorbitant. The emergence of self-supervised pretraining underscores the significance of leveraging unlabeled data for enhanced model performance. Integrating active learning with self-supervised pretraining allows the exploitation of vast amounts of unlabeled data in tandem with targeted annotation of crucial samples. 

Our investigation combines active learning and self-supervised pretraining in the context of ImageNet. The evaluation reveals that our approach surpasses conventional learning methods by 1.13% and 1.19% on top-1 and top-5 accuracy, respectively. The research also examines theoretical active learning scenarios that assume priority annotation of critically uncertain samples as measured by their prediction probabilities. Ultimately, our study provides industrial-scale companies with actionable guidelines that involve: 

1) employing a strong pretraining model capable of extracting diverse data samples;
2) leveraging batch selection algorithms that efficiently identify annotation candidates;
3) adopting reliable evaluation metrics to assess AL performance;
4) conducting thorough experiments with various evaluation metrics and data sets. 

The resulting framework demonstrates our proposed approach's capability to enhance model performance by 1.5% on the ResNet-50 benchmark. The code and guidelines are publicly available at https://github.com/google-research/vision-task-active-learning. The study's findings are applicable to other vision tasks

